WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.008 --> 00:00:07.550
MARIANNE SLIGHT:
What we want to talk

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:12.780
to you about this afternoon is
the truth about health data.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.670
So many of you, I'm sure, are
working with life science data

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:18.740
or with health care data.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.850
And for some of those
sources, it's quite OK.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:28.400
So EHR data now, generally,
is coming to us in a--

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.600
well, it's not quite a modern
format, is it, HL7, Version 2?

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.440
But we've got FHIR,
we've got HL7 Version 3.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.570
We have-- somebody
told me yesterday

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:44.840
it's now 40 years old, the
X12 claims format standard.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:45.660
And that's OK.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:47.540
We know how to deal with that.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:51.200
We now, in Google Cloud,
have a health care API

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.510
that's in beta that can bring
in data in HL7 Version 2

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:00.890
and FHIR in its native formats.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:06.470
Medical imaging data typically
comes in in DICOM format.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.370
Or pathology systems often have
a slightly different format

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.280
as well.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.450
But again, we recognize those,
we know those pretty well,

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:21.490
and we also have DICOM
support within our health API.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:24.380
Genomic data-- in health
care and life sciences,

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.590
we think about bringing all of
these different types of data,

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.580
different modalities
of data, together.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:35.510
And typically, we look
for different variants.

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:39.950
And so we bring in those
variant data in VCF files

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.230
directly into BigQuery.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.940
And that all works too.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.320
We have an API for that.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.340
That's fantastic.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.790
We've got a couple of
emerging types of data

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.530
that we're seeing more
and more commonly.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.890
And I know you guys are
starting to think about how

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.260
you're going to use that data.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.578
You know you want
to use that data.

00:01:59.578 --> 00:02:01.370
But the challenge with
that is that there's

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.100
no common standards yet
for that kind of data.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.970
And so that's something
that we're watching.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:11.090
The space will work to
do these kinds of tools

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.660
that we need to
ingest it over time.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.750
The one that's missing
here is that text data,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:23.360
that unstructured
clinical notes, the notes

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.690
that physicians write
as they're documenting

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:32.390
either your progress within an
acute stay, or discharge notes,

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.960
and so on.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:38.090
And we see that come through
in lots of different formats.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:40.910
Sometimes we see it
coming through the wire

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:43.790
on top of a PDF interface.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:45.650
And then we need
to think about how

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.490
you're going to OCR that, how
you're going to understand

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:52.580
what's really in that content.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.360
So the way that I like to think
about these different data sets

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.300
is from top to bottom.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:04.430
These are small to large
types of data sets.

00:03:04.430 --> 00:03:08.160
Genomics is absolutely enormous,
somewhere in the middle.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.920
But in terms of
messiness, I think

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.740
EHR data, and particularly
clinical notes data,

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:17.100
is some of the messiest.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.690
It's some of the hardest
to extract real value

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.900
from when you're trying to
integrate multiple sources

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:24.710
of that together.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.930
So let's talk a little bit
about those clinical notes.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:35.310
Here's a sample of a note from
a patient who was in the ICU.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.890
Notes are dense, right?

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:41.790
There's a lot of data
up there in that note.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:46.290
And in addition, there are
many, many abbreviations.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.870
And actually, often,
some of the abbreviations

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.580
are in Latin as well.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:54.390
And so it's not really
obvious around how

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.660
you go about extracting
the clinical meaning.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:03.620
And yet there's clearly a lot of
clinical meaning in this note.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:07.330
In addition, because
of the lovely vagaries

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:10.990
of our different EMR systems,
never mind the unwillingness

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:14.560
of our physicians to
learn to type well,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.019
we have all sorts of
punctuation issues.

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:19.220
There are spaces
that are missing.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.210
There are, in some cases,
very structured approaches

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:25.910
to using a semicolon
versus a comma.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:29.470
But each one is
different, and each note

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.600
from each different
institution often

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.040
has a very different format.

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:37.190
They're long.

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:40.490
This is an ICU note.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:43.430
No, I think this is
a neurology note.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:46.500
They're very long in many cases.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:51.540
And so that's a lot of
information to plow through.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.660
Sometimes they're
not unstructured.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.310
They contain structure.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.850
They contain tabular data.

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:01.950
And that can be hard for an
NLP system, a Natural Language

00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:06.030
Processing system, to really
understand, especially

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:10.230
when the raw data for this
table didn't look like this.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:15.130
What it looked like was more of
a bespoke structure like that.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:17.040
And what's interesting
about this--

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:20.760
so it's probably a bit hard
to see from down there,

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:25.020
but this is some lab data,
and the names of the labs,

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.230
I actually highlighted
in red here.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.000
The challenge with
this is that you'll

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.980
notice that there
are lots of gaps

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.980
in the table because
the data wasn't

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.480
fully dense for every
single time period.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.540
There wasn't a lab at
every single time period.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.720
And so sometimes you
have to interpolate

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:50.610
on which piece of data
belongs to which time period.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.970
That sort of semi-structured
data can be really difficult.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:57.660
And when we think about
semi-structured data,

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:02.100
it's not just tabular data
like this and other labs.

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.520
It also includes things
like understanding

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.020
notes sections,
where you might have,

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.090
for example, in a SOAP note--

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:17.560
a Subjective, Objective,
Assessment, and a Plan--

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.280
You might have different
types of note headers

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:23.520
that give you some
metadata about that note.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.870
But that, too, needs its
own special kind of parsing.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:31.590
Or you might also have
many different notes.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.600
You often have many
different notes

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:35.370
for a particular encounter.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:39.540
And the most common example
of this is within the ICU,

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:41.340
where patients
stay for typically

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.600
a longer period of time.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.370
The other challenge
that's increased

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:51.880
as we think about
electronic medical records

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:54.100
is that notes are
often copied forward.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.260
And this has definitely
been exacerbated

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:59.200
with the increase in
EMR usage, not least

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:03.100
because the meaningful
use regulations that

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:07.300
were so compelling in
terms of EMR adoption--

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.500
because they initially waived
the carrot of some money,

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.370
and then waved a stick
of taking money away--

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:18.250
forced the adoption
of an EMR system.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:21.760
But that EMR system
needed to be updated

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.270
with different regulatory
content every two years.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.870
And so the EMR vendors
really struggled to keep up.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:33.210
And so what went by the
wayside was usability.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:36.790
And so physicians got
fed up, got burned out.

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:40.840
And the EMR was introduced
ways to accelerate,

00:07:40.840 --> 00:07:44.060
for them, the entry of
that clinical findings,

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.918
the entry of those
clinical notes.

00:07:46.918 --> 00:07:48.460
And one of the ways
they did that was

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:51.580
with copy forward or
copy and paste, from one

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.930
note to another.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:57.640
This is from a
paper two years ago.

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.680
And the paper was
done based on data

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:06.130
at University of California
San Francisco Medical Center.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.920
It used Epic, but we
see the same problems

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.300
in every single EMR.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.940
I don't want to
pick on Epic here.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:14.132
In a typical note--

00:08:14.132 --> 00:08:16.090
oh, and I'm sorry, there's
a weird space there.

00:08:16.090 --> 00:08:17.720
That should be a comma.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:25.180
We analyzed 23,630 notes written
by almost 500 clinicians,

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:30.790
and found that, in a typical
note, almost 50% of that note

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:32.289
was copied.

00:08:32.289 --> 00:08:39.340
Another 36% was imported,
and only 18% of the text

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:41.260
was manually entered.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:42.970
That's a huge
amount, particularly

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:47.740
of the copied, copied forward,
copied from other places.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:53.050
And the graph here shows the
prevalence of progress notes

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.080
by the amount of
manually-entered text.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.710
So relatively little
fully manually-entered

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:05.830
text, a huge amount, with very
little manually-entered text.

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:07.820
So this is a problem.

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:10.120
It's a problem to
try and understand,

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:14.620
to pull out, the richness
of the information

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.080
that's buried in
that clinical note.

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:18.850
Because the clinical
note is supposed

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:22.900
to be the repository of the
physician's thinking process,

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:25.630
decision-making
process, the evidence

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:29.830
that they found, how they did
the differential diagnosis,

00:09:29.830 --> 00:09:32.780
and what the treatment
plan is for the patient.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:36.190
And that decision-making
process isn't really

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:39.260
captured anywhere else.

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:43.030
So it's really important
to be able to mine

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:44.950
the wealth of this
information that's

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:50.560
buried in the data
of the clinical note.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:53.030
So what are we doing about it?

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:56.560
And when you think about
ingesting health care and life

00:09:56.560 --> 00:10:03.130
science data, how do you think
about getting the information

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:08.260
out of that data, aligned into
a single environment for you

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:12.430
to use as the basis of
analysis for machine learning?

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:16.690
Well, when we think about it,
after that ingestion piece,

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:19.600
where will you bring your
data onto Google Cloud,

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.040
the next thing
that we think about

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.510
is how you harmonize the data
from those different sources

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:31.030
that you have so that it all
speaks the same language.

00:10:31.030 --> 00:10:33.100
And the way that
we think about that

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.310
is from a historical data
perspective, and then

00:10:36.310 --> 00:10:39.250
incremental and
streaming perspectives.

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:43.120
Because when you think about
bringing data onto cloud,

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.690
usually you're starting with
something that exists already,

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:49.340
whether that's a
pile of CSV files,

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:52.400
whether that's an archival
database that you already have,

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:56.740
whether it's an existing CDW
or an existing data lake,

00:10:56.740 --> 00:11:01.780
or maybe an existing
Clarity database, et cetera.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:05.380
And so bringing that
historical data in

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:07.480
is very similar, in
a way, to bringing

00:11:07.480 --> 00:11:10.180
in the incremental
or the streaming data

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.860
from the harmonization
perspective.

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:14.410
Not from the
ingestion perspective,

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:16.910
but from the
harmonization perspective.

00:11:16.910 --> 00:11:20.140
It's just there are some
different formats as well.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.270
And I'm focusing, in
this picture here,

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:24.940
on data from electronic
health records.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:29.230
But equally, this could include
DICOM data from imaging,

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:35.290
and it could include VCF file
data, as well, from genomics.

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:38.950
From an incremental data
perspective, as well as

00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:43.360
those snapshot increments of
CSV, or pulling it directly

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:45.550
from another database,
more commonly,

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:48.820
with health care data,
that incremental data

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:55.120
is HL7 messages, or
FHIR messages as well.

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:58.330
And the transformations
that we need to do

00:11:58.330 --> 00:12:01.670
are really in these four forms.

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:04.850
The first is field mappings.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:08.980
So you've got maybe five
hospitals, let's say--

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:13.300
maybe 105 hospitals,
on one side.

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:17.120
And not every single one of
them are using a different EMR.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.900
But I'll bet you many of them
are using a different EMR

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:24.550
instance, which is configured
differently from the next EMR

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:28.360
instance, and often they
are using different EMRs.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:30.565
Even in very large
health systems--

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:34.840
I've been talking
to one recently

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:38.410
that has more than 50 hospitals.

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:43.930
They still have Epic, they have
Meditech, they have Allscripts.

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:45.640
And those Epic instances--

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:46.980
there's a lot of them--

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:50.480
they're all
differently configured.

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:53.583
So as you bring
in that data, what

00:12:53.583 --> 00:12:55.000
that means is that
you've got lots

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:59.600
of different schemas of
source coming into the system.

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:03.250
And so how do you want
to align that data?

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:06.130
Do you want to align it to FHIR?

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:08.290
Do you have a schema
in mind already

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:11.230
that you want to align it to?

00:13:11.230 --> 00:13:13.780
Is it one of the
standard schemas?

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:17.890
Unfortunately, there's a
plethora of common data models,

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.890
such as OMOP, which
is commonly used

00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:25.900
within research organizations
and within pharma

00:13:25.900 --> 00:13:29.590
companies, CDISC, which
you need from a pharma

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:35.650
perspective for things like us
STDM, i2b2, or your own custom

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:37.550
schema.

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:41.170
So what we're trying to do, from
a harmonization perspective,

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:44.800
is understand these
target schemas

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:49.210
as first-class health care
data citizens in the same way

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:56.440
that we understand FHIR,
and HL7, and DICOM, and VCF

00:13:56.440 --> 00:14:01.160
as first-class data
transmission citizens.

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:03.940
And if you don't want to use
one of these first-class data

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:05.650
citizens, you don't have to.

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:09.280
If you've got a schema that
you're particularly keen on,

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:10.240
that works, too.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:13.270
But what's important
is aligning the data

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:19.510
from all your different sources
into that single target.

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:23.200
And how you do that is
really with these four types

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:24.920
of transformation.

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:27.500
So first of all,
the field mappings.

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:31.510
When I say Pat ID
up here, and then I

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:37.360
say PT underscore ID here,
and then I say MRN over there,

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.970
I need to know that that
goes to Patient underscore

00:14:39.970 --> 00:14:42.490
ID over here.

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:44.750
That's very simplistic.

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:47.960
The second area is around
terminology mappings.

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:51.350
Because often, those
same multiplicity

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:55.310
of different sources also
use different terminologies

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:57.960
for the same concept.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:03.470
For example, this hospital uses
RxNorm as its primary dimension

00:15:03.470 --> 00:15:07.910
table for medications, but
this hospital over there

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:11.570
uses FDB, and that
one uses [? Multim. ?]

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:15.650
And so that's just an example
on the medication side.

00:15:15.650 --> 00:15:20.510
When you think about extensions
that groups have made--

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:22.700
whether that's
regional extensions,

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:26.420
whether that's versions
of terminologies

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:29.480
that people are using
for different purposes--

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:31.070
all of those
terminology mappings

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:32.940
need to happen as well.

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:36.050
And so far, we're just talking
about the structured data

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:37.820
for data harmonization,
but I'm going

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:41.420
to come to the unstructured
text in a moment.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:45.170
The third area of mapping
transformation is units.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:47.120
Because when you're
in a NICU, and you're

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:51.140
measuring the weight,
the mass of a neonate,

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:53.480
typically, that's done in grams.

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:58.460
But when you're measuring the
weight of an adult in a CV ICU,

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:03.260
for example, that's typically
done in pounds or kilos.

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:06.380
And so making sure that
that units alignment happens

00:16:06.380 --> 00:16:11.240
is really critical to being able
to use the data for analytics

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:12.630
or machine learning.

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:17.790
And it's not just weight, it's
also all those labs as well.

00:16:17.790 --> 00:16:19.670
And then the fourth
type, which is the type

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:22.580
that I'm going to spend the
rest of this talk focusing on,

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:24.920
is clinical entity extraction.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:30.740
Understanding, extracting
out, those clinical concepts

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:36.890
and the items within
their own ontologies

00:16:36.890 --> 00:16:41.270
into structured data, and
augmenting the structured data

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:42.650
with those items.

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:47.290
But there are
different approaches

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:48.530
for different use cases.

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:51.120
And this is where NLP becomes
really, really interesting,

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:52.180
I think.

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:55.030
So from a physician's
perspective,

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:58.870
what they want to do
is have a system that

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:01.990
plows through their
clinical notes

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:05.470
and extracts out all of
those clinical entities

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.230
that they were just busy
dictating or busy typing

00:17:08.230 --> 00:17:15.220
into their clinical note, and
have those added into the EMR

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:19.160
as structured data,
or at least have them

00:17:19.160 --> 00:17:25.700
added into the clinical
warehouse for analytics.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:29.920
But the rules that they use
for determining what to extract

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:33.710
and how to code those
is very different--

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:36.280
it really is-- from the
rules that a medical coder

00:17:36.280 --> 00:17:40.540
must follow in order for the
hospital to be reimbursed

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:43.580
or for the physician
to be reimbursed.

00:17:43.580 --> 00:17:46.390
So if you look at
the coder training,

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:49.630
they don't go through x many
years of medical school.

00:17:49.630 --> 00:17:53.320
Instead, they go through x
many years or months of AHIMA

00:17:53.320 --> 00:17:55.570
training, and then
they're delivered

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:58.120
with a book that's about
that thick that's the coding

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:02.140
guidelines, updated every
year, that lands on their desk.

00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:06.070
And those rules are different
from what a physician thinks

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:09.640
about in terms of the way
that different diagnoses are

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:11.560
defined, for example.

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:15.400
So sepsis means one
thing to a physician,

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:18.550
and it means something entirely
different to a medical coder--

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:21.640
not entirely different,
but different enough.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:25.980
Those are different rules
that they must follow.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:28.890
From a CDI perspective,
a Clinical Documentation

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:33.120
Improvement specialist,
their job is to,

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:36.630
from an inpatient perspective,
go through the documentation

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:42.780
and look for opportunities where
there is clinical evidence that

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:45.730
suggests a different diagnosis.

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:51.030
So that's another use case
for this clinical entity

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:53.020
extraction.

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:55.495
And from the
payer's perspective,

00:18:55.495 --> 00:18:57.120
they want to look at
the clinical notes

00:18:57.120 --> 00:19:02.070
in order to identify any
fraud, waste, or abuse.

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:04.320
And so again, that's a
different perspective.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:07.320
And as any of you who've been
using machine learning know,

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:09.960
there's a large
difference between models

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:15.460
that you create for compliance
versus for discovery.

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:19.370
So one size does not
fit all in this case,

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:25.880
and one NLP model does not fit
all, either, because of this.

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:29.670
So maybe-- there we go.

00:19:29.670 --> 00:19:32.340
I'm sorry if I was
blinding somebody.

00:19:32.340 --> 00:19:34.890
Let's talk, then, about our
approach to natural language

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:35.550
processing.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.590
And I want to say this
is a preview of what

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:41.550
we're building here at Google.

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:44.860
But we're going to
follow these five steps.

00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:48.300
So in order to extract
that clinical information

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:50.430
for a patient,
we're starting off

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:51.880
with named entity recognition.

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:53.910
And named entity
recognition means

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:57.270
scanning all of
that text, finding

00:19:57.270 --> 00:20:02.080
all of those clinical entities,
finding all of the concepts.

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:05.430
And this is a task that is very
suitable for machine learning.

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:08.790
All it requires is
lots and lots of data,

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:10.560
and data that is labeled--

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:12.300
lots of labeling as well--

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:19.200
to identify not just
the clinical concepts

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.630
themselves, but also
where it found them

00:20:21.630 --> 00:20:24.870
in the document, the
positional information.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:27.240
That needs a lot of
data because we have

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.340
a lot of medical specialties.

00:20:29.340 --> 00:20:32.400
We have a huge amount
now of ICD-10 codes,

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:35.580
including procedure codes,
and a huge amount of things

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:40.210
like LOINC codes as well, which
leads me on to my second piece.

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:41.724
Ooh, where did that go?

00:20:41.724 --> 00:20:44.328
Ooh, come back.

00:20:44.328 --> 00:20:47.130
There we go.

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:47.830
Sorry.

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:48.890
Oh, let's go back one.

00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:54.090
My builds didn't work, did they?

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:59.310
The second piece is mapping
those named entity recognitions

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:05.370
into the ontology, and then
into the item classification

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:08.430
for each of those
medical concepts.

00:21:08.430 --> 00:21:13.170
So you need to decide, when
you're doing natural language

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:16.935
processing and you're
extracting out medications,

00:21:16.935 --> 00:21:18.060
what do you want to map to?

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.100
Do you want to map
to FDB because that's

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:22.360
what you use within
your health system?

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:26.580
Do you want to map to RxNorm
because that's typically

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:31.750
what everybody else is
using around for analytics?

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:33.750
Or do you want to use
[? Multim ?] because maybe

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:37.020
you've got some medication
interaction tracking system

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:39.990
that's based on [? Multim? ?]

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:42.330
So you need to
understand, once you've

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:44.850
identified which
ontology you want to use,

00:21:44.850 --> 00:21:47.670
which concept each
entity belongs to.

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:51.580
And this is sometimes more
complicated than it sounds,

00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:53.680
and I'll give you an example.

00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:58.530
So when you say something
like penicillin,

00:21:58.530 --> 00:22:02.130
that could mean a
medication order,

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:05.530
or it could mean a
medication allergy.

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:08.160
And so you need to
start understanding

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:11.910
the difference between those,
and choosing the right concept

00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:15.660
that the entity belongs to, and
then choosing the right item

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:18.330
within that concept list.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:22.260
Again, extensive training data,
but fundamentally a machine

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:25.620
language problem.

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:30.580
OK, now let's try
for number 3, maybe.

00:22:30.580 --> 00:22:33.960
OK, number 3 is
the semantic model.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:37.670
It's all very well to be able
to extract out those entities,

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:43.280
map them to what the ontology
is and the terminology you

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:44.940
want to use.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:45.860
But that's not enough.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:47.652
Because what about when
the physician says,

00:22:47.652 --> 00:22:54.370
rule out chest pneumonia,
rule out something else,

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:56.130
some other diagnosis?

00:22:56.130 --> 00:23:00.840
Patient does not have
blah-dy-blah, so negation.

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:03.090
You need to start thinking
about a semantic model that

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:05.743
handles that negation,
that handles what I always

00:23:05.743 --> 00:23:09.420
like to say, the fear,
uncertainty, and doubt.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:14.170
"Could be" doesn't mean
the patient has it.

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:16.720
Doing a differential
diagnosis, understanding

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:21.310
the typical language that's used
for a differential diagnosis,

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:25.900
understanding the
difference between time--

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:31.970
between patient had
pneumonia three months ago,

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:35.650
but that's since been resolved--
versus this patient has

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:36.910
pneumonia.

00:23:36.910 --> 00:23:39.520
Understanding who
has the entity.

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:42.840
Patient's mother died
of a heart attack.

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:45.175
That heart attack
doesn't accrue.

00:23:45.175 --> 00:23:48.430
It doesn't get assigned
to the patient.

00:23:48.430 --> 00:23:53.260
It gets assigned as a
part of a family history.

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:56.600
And that's the simple
types of semantic model.

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:59.380
There are other, more
complicated types.

00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:05.320
Things like chest pain, in the
presence of something else,

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:08.240
might lead you to a
different diagnosis.

00:24:08.240 --> 00:24:11.140
And so those are
two different areas

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:14.620
that need to be handled within
natural language processing

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:17.310
and clinical entity extraction.

00:24:17.310 --> 00:24:19.170
I see it's done it again.

00:24:19.170 --> 00:24:23.290
Ah, I apologize.

00:24:23.290 --> 00:24:28.900
Number 4 is the
context of the patient.

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:31.630
One note doesn't give you all
the history of the patient,

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:32.470
does it?

00:24:32.470 --> 00:24:36.070
And sometimes you
need more information

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:38.050
from the rest of
the note in order

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:40.310
to understand what to extract.

00:24:40.310 --> 00:24:42.910
And this is particularly common
within the medical coding

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:44.180
example.

00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:48.860
In medical coding, you want
all of the notes associated

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:53.770
for that patient from
this particular encounter,

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:56.320
and yet not all of the notes.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:59.350
What you want to do--

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:00.550
ah, come back.

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:03.880
I do apologize.

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:04.890
There we go.

00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:07.770
You also need to take in
the context of the use case.

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:10.770
Because for medical coding,
it's not all note types,

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:14.910
it's only certain note
types for this encounter.

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:19.050
And so you need to really grok
the context of that broader use

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:22.540
case, understand what
the guidelines are,

00:25:22.540 --> 00:25:26.190
and then make sure that
that is appropriate,

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:31.170
that you're building a flow
that is appropriate for that.

00:25:31.170 --> 00:25:34.620
And so any additional ontologies
that you need to layer in,

00:25:34.620 --> 00:25:37.800
any sections of notes,
parsing the notes to make

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:39.588
the different sections,
and then doing,

00:25:39.588 --> 00:25:41.880
of course, all the wiring up
of getting the notes there

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:43.990
in the first place.

00:25:43.990 --> 00:25:46.410
So when we think about
natural language processing

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:50.220
and clinical entity
extraction, these

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.170
are the five areas
that we're looking at.

00:25:52.170 --> 00:25:55.080
And what we're
working on right now

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:59.160
is the ability to
provide an API that

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:04.410
would allow you to do the
first portions of this.

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:08.440
To us, named entity
recognition is insufficient.

00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:12.300
That doesn't help you
dramatically on its own.

00:26:12.300 --> 00:26:16.680
What you need to do is include
the concept classification

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:20.610
and the semantic model in
order to have clinical entity

00:26:20.610 --> 00:26:22.050
extraction.

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:24.765
And so now what I'd like
to do is talk a little bit

00:26:24.765 --> 00:26:26.640
about a couple of other
items, and then we're

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:28.840
going to move to a demo.

00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:32.370
So the first piece
is around tuning,

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:41.910
because every health system has
different unique documentation

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:43.560
practices.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:48.265
And so in some cases, they'll
all go with a SOAP note.

00:26:48.265 --> 00:26:49.890
That's not very common
that they'll all

00:26:49.890 --> 00:26:51.600
go with a SOAP note.

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:55.123
But in other places, they'll
have a particular structure

00:26:55.123 --> 00:26:56.040
that they want to use.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:27:00.480
And often, that
differs by specialty.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:02.490
And also, often,
things like how they

00:27:02.490 --> 00:27:04.590
use abbreviations
is very different,

00:27:04.590 --> 00:27:07.800
and is driven by
the health system

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:11.660
as some kind of
overall guideline.

00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:13.830
There is often a
unique use of terms.

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:18.570
I remember working
with a system that

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:24.720
often used the abbreviation
OB, which one NLP system took

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:26.820
to mean obstetrics.

00:27:26.820 --> 00:27:28.680
And actually, they
meant obesity.

00:27:28.680 --> 00:27:30.760
The patient had obesity.

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:33.600
And that, we only discovered
when we started looking really

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:36.990
at the data quality issues.

00:27:36.990 --> 00:27:39.180
There are often
unique guidelines

00:27:39.180 --> 00:27:41.880
because, for example, some
organizations will take

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:44.650
a different approach to risk.

00:27:44.650 --> 00:27:49.140
And so that will tweak
the model, particularly,

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:54.170
when you're looking at things
from a compliance perspective.

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:58.240
So let's take a
look at the demo.

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:00.670
Just before we
switch there, I want

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:02.770
to give you a brief
introduction to the demo

00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:03.890
that we're going to show.

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:05.080
So this is a preview.

00:28:05.080 --> 00:28:07.786
This isn't available today.

00:28:07.786 --> 00:28:11.350
And what we're
going to show you is

00:28:11.350 --> 00:28:16.540
how you will be able to
integrate any existing NLP

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:19.840
solution you have
with Google Cloud,

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:24.400
and extend it by leveraging
Google Cloud's capabilities as

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:25.940
well.

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:28.150
And so, fundamentally,
what we're going to do

00:28:28.150 --> 00:28:32.140
is show you a Jupyter
Notebook, which

00:28:32.140 --> 00:28:35.500
is a collaborative
analytics notebook.

00:28:35.500 --> 00:28:37.520
It's connected to BigQuery.

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:39.430
And we're going to show
you two different NLP

00:28:39.430 --> 00:28:43.360
models and how they can
be integrated together.

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:45.460
One NLP model that
we're going to show

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.060
is one that many
of you, I'm sure,

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:49.090
are familiar with,
which is cTAKES.

00:28:49.090 --> 00:28:51.520
It's an open-source NLP model.

00:28:51.520 --> 00:28:55.930
The other is one of our Google
research models for NLP.

00:28:55.930 --> 00:29:00.190
It's a research model
that is clinically based.

00:29:00.190 --> 00:29:03.310
And the reason that I've got
all of these items differently

00:29:03.310 --> 00:29:05.080
colored is because
you're going to start

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:08.110
to see different
concepts extracted

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:10.730
by the different models.

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:14.440
So for example, in
the cTAKES model,

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:18.100
we are using coding
for that to SNOMED

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:20.780
and medications to RxNorm.

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:22.780
But the research model
that we're going to use

00:29:22.780 --> 00:29:25.970
goes a lot deeper on
the medication side,

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:29.680
and it also identifies
different types of PHI.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:31.675
So I won't remember
these colors.

00:29:31.675 --> 00:29:33.550
You don't have to remember
the colors either.

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:36.440
But we'll point out
some of the differences.

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:39.340
So if we could switch
to the demo, please.

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:40.940
Great.

00:29:40.940 --> 00:29:43.940
So the first steps in here--

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:46.150
so this is a Jupyter Notebook.

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:47.870
The first steps that
we're going to do

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:51.940
are just to import all of
the libraries in Python,

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:54.400
and start loading up
all of the gubbins

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:57.980
that we need in order
to run NLP here.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:29:59.410
And that's done.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:01.720
And now we're going to--

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:03.520
I'm reading from
the screen here--

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:06.100
load up the notes and the
annotations from BigQuery.

00:30:06.100 --> 00:30:09.088
Because in this case,
we're using this directly

